---

<Article
  title="Arch vs. Straight Wall"
  eyebrow="Guide"
  description="The trade-off between arch and straight-wall steel buildings: cost, usable floor space, door placement and how to pick the right one."
  lede="This is the first real decision in a steel building project, and it constrains most of the ones that follow. Neither is better in the abstract — they fail in different places."
  crumbs={[{ label: 'Resources', href: '/resources/' }]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="how">How each one works</h2>
  <p>
    An <strong>arch building</strong> is a continuous curve of corrugated steel bolted rib to
    rib. The shape is the structure — there is no separate frame holding up a separate skin.
    That is why it uses less steel per square foot than almost any other way of enclosing
    space, and why the interior is completely free of columns.
  </p>
  <p>
    A <strong>straight-wall building</strong> separates those jobs. A frame carries the load,
    panels close it in, and the walls stand vertical. It costs more in material and labor
    because you are building two systems instead of one, and it buys you something specific
    in return.
  </p>

  <h2 id="floor">The floor space question</h2>
  <p>
    This is where the decision is actually made. In an arch, the walls curve inward as they
    approach the floor. The square footage on the drawing is real, but the space near the
    perimeter has limited headroom. A 30' wide arch does not give you 30' of wall you can
    stand a cabinet against.
  </p>
  <p>
    For storage of things that are low, or for equipment parked down the centerline, this
    costs you nothing. For a workshop where you wanted benches, shelving and a lumber rack
    around the perimeter, it costs you the whole reason you built it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The practical test:</strong> walk your intended layout and ask what lives against
    the walls. If the answer is "nothing much," buy the arch and keep the money. If the answer
    is a bench wall, racking or tall storage, the straight wall pays for itself immediately.
  </p>

  <h2 id="cost">Cost</h2>
  <p>
    Arches are meaningfully cheaper per square foot at comparable spans, and the gap is
    widest at small and medium sizes. They are also faster to assemble, because there are
    fewer distinct components and no separate framing stage — which matters if you are
    raising it yourself.
  </p>
  <p>
    At very large spans the comparison narrows, and above a certain size wide-span structural
    profiles become the sensible answer regardless, because they handle the loads and the
    door requirements that big buildings bring.
  </p>

  <h2 id="doors">Doors and openings</h2>
  <p>
    Straight walls take doors and windows in conventional positions, including along the
    sidewall, because there is a full-height vertical plane to put them in. This matters more
    than it sounds: multiple overhead doors along one side is straightforward in a
    straight-wall building and awkward in an arch.
  </p>
  <p>
    In an arch, openings are simplest in the endwalls. A sidewall opening means framing into
    the curve itself, which is possible but constrains size and placement. If your layout
    depends on side access, that is a strong signal.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pick">How to choose</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Choose an arch</strong> when cost per square foot is the priority, the contents are low or centered, and endwall access is fine.</li>
    <li><strong>Choose a straight wall</strong> when you need usable wall height, sidewall doors, or the building will be finished as occupied space.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider the hybrid</strong> — straight sidewalls with an arched roof — when you want most of the arch's structural efficiency and still need real wall height. It is the most common recommendation for people torn between the two.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If you are still unsure, describe what goes inside and where it sits. That usually settles
    it faster than comparing specifications.
  </p>
</Article>
